    id: "cons-2025-hl-q8-complete", topicId: "cons-6-5", conceptId: "airtight-envelope-complete",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2025 HL Q8 — complete",
    stem: "An airtight external building envelope is important in the design of a dwelling house.",
    questionText: "(a) Discuss in detail two benefits of preventing air leakage from a dwelling house. (b) The drawing shows an outline section of a two-storey house having a 450 mm external concrete block wall with a 250 mm full-fill insulated cavity. The house has a truss roof and a first floor of timber construction. The external door has a thermally broken frame. A Mechanical Ventilation with Heat Recovery system supplies ventilation through ceiling ducts. Using notes and freehand sketches, show best practice design detailing that will prevent air leakage at any three of the locations circled on the drawing.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 60,
    rows: [
    { id: "a-benefit-1", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Benefit 1 (3 for point, 3 for discussion)", marks: 6, contextNote: "Point 3 and discussion 3. Such as: lower energy bills because less heat escapes through gaps in the building fabric; lower carbon emissions from heating.", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3,4,5,6], parts: [{"id":"point","verbatim":"point","marks":3,"permittedMarks":[0,1,2,3]},{"id":"discussion","verbatim":"discussion","marks":3,"permittedMarks":[0,1,2,3]}] },
    { id: "a-benefit-2", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Benefit 2 (3 for point, 3 for discussion)", marks: 6, contextNote: "A second, different benefit: point 3 and discussion 3. Such as: mechanical ventilation works efficiently with better indoor air quality; no draughts; less moisture entering the fabric (preventing rot); less noise; the insulation performs better.", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3,4,5,6], parts: [{"id":"point","verbatim":"point","marks":3,"permittedMarks":[0,1,2,3]},{"id":"discussion","verbatim":"discussion","marks":3,"permittedMarks":[0,1,2,3]}] },
    { id: "b-locations", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "Best practice design detailing to prevent air leakage at any three of the locations circled", marks: 48, contextNote: "Any three of the four circled locations, each notes 8 and sketches 8 = 16, showing the continuous airtight layer. Head of external door: seal the thermally broken door frame to the reveal of the inner block wall with plasterable airtightness tape, then plaster over the tape; or airtightness paint on the reveal before plastering, or slabbing and skimming. First floor junction with external wall: make the wall airtight where the joists bear — an airtightness membrane sealed with airtight silicone or plasterable tape, or sand cement render or airtightness paint; tape each joist where it breaks the airtightness barrier; seal the floor/wall and roof/wall junctions with tape or paint. Wallplate level: an airtightness membrane under the ceiling joists, wrapped onto the wallplate and down onto the first block below it, fixed to the wall with plasterable airtightness tape or a bead of airtight silicone, then scrim or mesh and sand cement plaster. MVHR duct protruding through the ceiling: an airtightness membrane under the joists, sealed to the duct with a self-adhesive grommet fixed to the membrane, or by carefully taping the duct to the membrane with airtightness tape.", group: {"claimMax":3,"perOption":16,"options":["Head of external door","First floor junction with external wall","Wallplate level","MVHR duct protruding through the ceiling"]} },
    ],
