    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Construction Studies 2020 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "cons-2020-hl-q5-complete", topicId: "cons-6-1", conceptId: "ground-floor-u-value-complete",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2020 HL Q5 — complete",
    stem: "A house built to nZEB standards has a highly insulated solid concrete ground floor with a hardwood finish, as shown.",
    questionText: "(a) Calculate the U-value of the floor, given the construction has the following sequence and data: Hardwood flooring thickness 20 mm; sand/cement fine screed thickness 65 mm; floor insulation thickness 200mm; concrete floor slab thickness 150 mm; radon barrier thickness 0.25 mm; sand blinding thickness 40 mm; hardcore thickness 200 mm; subsoil thickness 300 mm. Thermal data of the ground floor: resistance of internal top surface of floor (R) 0.104 m2 ⁰C/W; conductivity of hardwood flooring (k) 6.666 W/m ⁰C; resistivity of fine screed (r) 1.410 m ⁰C/W; conductivity of floor insulation (k) 0.022 W/m ⁰C; conductivity of concrete floor slab (k) 1.280 W/m ⁰C; conductivity of radon barrier (k) 0.250 W/m ⁰C; conductivity of sand blinding (k) 0.160 W/m ⁰C; conductivity of hardcore (k) 1.350 W/m ⁰C; conductivity of subsoil (k) 1.600 W/m ⁰C. (b) Using the U-value of the floor obtained at 5(a) above and the following data, calculate the cost of heat lost annually through this floor: dimensions of floor slab 6.5 metres × 9.0 metres; average internal temperature 20 ⁰C; average temperature of subsoil 6 ⁰C; heating period 9 hours daily for 39 weeks per annum; cost of oil 96 cent per litre; calorific value of oil 37350 kJ per litre; 1000 Watts: 1 kJ per second. (c) Using notes and a freehand sketch, show best practice design detailing that will prevent the formation of a thermal bridge at the junction of the concrete floor and an external concrete block wall with a full-fill insulated cavity.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 60,
    rows: [
    { id: "a-resistances", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Formulae: R=T/k R=T × r", marks: 27, contextNote: "Nine resistances, 3 each, by R = T/k (or R = T × r for the screed): internal surface 0.104 (given); hardwood 0.02/6.666 = 0.003; screed 0.065 × 1.410 = 0.092; insulation 0.2/0.022 = 9.091; slab 0.15/1.28 = 0.117; radon barrier 0.00025/0.25 = 0.001; sand blinding 0.04/0.16 = 0.25; hardcore 0.2/1.35 = 0.148; subsoil 0.3/1.6 = 0.188. All in m²°C/W.", permittedMarks: [0,3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,27] },
    { id: "a-total-resistance", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Total resistance (R)", marks: 3, contextNote: "Total resistance Rt = 9.993 m²°C/W.", permittedMarks: [0,3] },
    { id: "a-u-value", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Calculation of U- value", marks: 3, contextNote: "U = 1 ÷ Rt = 1 ÷ 9.993 = 0.10 W/m²°C, with units.", permittedMarks: [0,3] },
    { id: "b-heat-loss", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Heat loss formula and calculations", marks: 3, contextNote: "Heat loss = U-value × area × temperature difference = 0.10 × 58.5 × (20 − 6) = 81.9 watts (joules per second).", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3] },
    { id: "b-duration", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Heating duration for one year", marks: 3, contextNote: "Heating period = 60 × 60 × 9 × 7 × 39 = 8,845,200 seconds (2,457 hours).", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3] },
    { id: "b-energy", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "k/Joules calculation for one year", marks: 3, contextNote: "Energy = 8,845,200 × 81.9 ÷ 1000 ≈ 724,422 kJ per year.", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3] },
    { id: "b-oil", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Litres of oil for one year", marks: 3, contextNote: "Oil = 724,421.88 ÷ 37,350 = 19.39 litres per year.", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3] },
    { id: "b-cost", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Annual cost of heat loss", marks: 3, contextNote: "Annual cost = 19.39 × €0.96 = €18.62. The scheme’s alternative one-line method gives the same €18.62.", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3] },
    { id: "c-notes", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Notes", marks: 4, contextNote: "Notes on the design detail, as in the scheme’s model: the floor and cavity insulation made continuous at the junction, with an insulated block or perimeter insulation.", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3,4] },
    { id: "c-sketch", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Sketch", marks: 8, contextNote: "Freehand sketch of the floor and cavity-wall junction.", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8] },
    ],
    taskGroups: [{"sourceCardId":"cons-2020-hl-q5-a","label":"a","marks":33,"rowIds":["a-resistances","a-total-resistance","a-u-value"]},{"sourceCardId":"cons-2020-hl-q5-b","label":"b","marks":15,"rowIds":["b-heat-loss","b-duration","b-energy","b-oil","b-cost"]},{"sourceCardId":"cons-2020-hl-q5-c","label":"c","marks":12,"rowIds":["c-notes","c-sketch"]}],
