    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Construction Studies 2019 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "cons-2019-hl-q4-b", topicId: "cons-1-1", conceptId: "townhouse-refurbishment-b",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2019 HL Q4(b)",
    stem: "The sketch shows a row of terraced townhouses built over 100 years ago. A young couple have purchased one of the houses and have decided to refurbish it as their family home.",
    questionText: "The owners commissioned a survey of the house which revealed the following: traditional cut roof with natural slates; softwood sliding sash windows with single-glazing; solid external walls of brick construction, uninsulated and with internal lime render. Select any two of the above areas and, using notes and freehand sketches, describe in detail the steps involved in upgrading each area selected in a manner that respects the appearance and character of the original townhouses.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 36,
    rows: [
    { id: "areas", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "Two areas for upgrading", marks: 36, contextNote: "Any two of the three printed areas, each notes 10 and sketches 8 = 18. Traditional cut roof with natural slates: remove the slates carefully, clean only their undersides of lime parging, weigh and grade them and refit the heaviest at the eaves; replace decayed rafter ends, wallplate and fascia in the same species and profiles; a vapour-diffuse breathable membrane with counter battens; cast-iron rainwater goods; hygroscopic insulation such as sheep’s wool with woodfibre board, and a thoroughly ventilated roof. Softwood sliding sash windows with single - glazing: repair rather than replace, by a specialist conservation joiner; remove the sashes and glass carefully and record their positions; replace rotten timber in the same species with traditional joints and animal glue; free the pulleys, renew the cords and reuse the counter-balance weights; non-toxic paint; discreet secondary glazing. Solid external walls of brick construction, uninsulated and with internal lime render: never cement mortar; have the lime mortar analysed and matched (NHL strength, sand); rake out by hand to twice the joint width and repoint in stages, protected with hessian; internally, hempcrete or an insulating lime plaster system with a three-coat lime render.", group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":18,"options":["Traditional cut roof with natural slates","Softwood sliding sash windows with single - glazing","Solid external walls of brick construction, uninsulated and with internal lime render"]} },
    ],
    sourceMaterial: {
          "kind": "source-illustration",
          "label": "ORIGINAL QUESTION 4",
          "title": "Refurbishing a terraced townhouse",
          "pages": [
                1,
                3
          ],
          "attribution": "SEC Leaving Certificate Construction Studies 2019 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
          "presentationNote": "Original sketch retained. Part (b) is a closed choice of any two of the three printed areas: one card holding all three."
    },
