    id: "cons-2013-hl-q9-complete", topicId: "cons-3-4", conceptId: "damp-junctions-complete",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2013 HL Q9 — complete",
    stem: "A two-storey house, as shown in the drawing, is of timber frame construction with a rendered concrete block outer leaf. The chimney is also of rendered concrete block construction and both roofs are slated. Careful design detailing is required to prevent the penetration of dampness at the critical junctions circled in the drawing.",
    questionText: "(a) Select any three locations from those circled on the drawing and show, using notes and freehand sketches, the typical design detailing which will prevent the penetration of dampness at each location. (b) Select any two junctions and specify a damp-proofing material suitable for each junction. Discuss the advantages of each material for the specified junction. (c) Discuss in detail the importance of ensuring that moisture does not penetrate to the inner leaf of a wall of timber frame construction.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 60,
    rows: [
    { id: "a-locations", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "Three locations", marks: 36, contextNote: "Any three of the four circled locations, each note 6 and sketch 6 = 12. Location 1: the chimney on the main roof: a lead back gutter welted under the slates; the roofing membrane carried under the battens and taped to the chimney; a cover flashing at the back of the chimney overhanging the lead gutter. Location 2: the window head: a stepped DPC so moisture cannot reach the inside leaf; an anti-capillary groove at the sash opening; a weather strip at the top of the window; mastic sealant around the window. Location 3: the window cill: the cill wrapped in DPC; a second layer of DPC around the cavity barrier; an anti-capillary groove in the cill; mastic sealant; airtight tape between the frame and the internal wall. Location 4: the lean-to roof against the wall: a stepped DPC; a cover flashing at the roof–wall junction; the roof membrane carried onto the wall and taped to the masonry.", group: {"claimMax":3,"perOption":12,"options":["Location 1","Location 2","Location 3","Location 4"]} },
    { id: "b-material-1", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Damp proof material 1 (specification 3 marks) (advantages 3 marks)", marks: 6, contextNote: "Specification 3 and advantages 3. Such as: lead — widely used around chimneys; flexible, corrosion-resistant, accommodates moderate movement, readily available and easily worked.", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3,4,5,6], parts: [{"id":"specification","verbatim":"specification","marks":3,"permittedMarks":[0,1,2,3]},{"id":"advantages","verbatim":"advantages","marks":3,"permittedMarks":[0,1,2,3]}] },
    { id: "b-material-2", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Damp proof material 2 (specification 3 marks) (advantages 3 marks)", marks: 6, contextNote: "A material for the second junction: specification 3 and advantages 3. Such as: a flexible plastic DPC — readily available, inexpensive and highly resistant to corrosive materials in mortar.", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3,4,5,6], parts: [{"id":"specification","verbatim":"specification","marks":3,"permittedMarks":[0,1,2,3]},{"id":"advantages","verbatim":"advantages","marks":3,"permittedMarks":[0,1,2,3]}] },
    { id: "c-discussion-1", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Discussion 1", marks: 6, contextNote: "Such as: a longer life, avoiding decay of the timber frame — fungal decay follows prolonged contact with damp masonry; damp insulation loses its effectiveness.", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3,4,5,6] },
    { id: "c-discussion-2", kind: "criterion", verbatim: "Discussion 2", marks: 6, contextNote: "A second, different point. Such as: no mould on the internal walls; openings detailed and sealed; the breather membrane fixed correctly so moisture is kept out while vapour escapes; timber kept away from masonry.", permittedMarks: [0,1,2,3,4,5,6] },
    ],
